speechtext:
Madam Speaker, I rise on a point of order. An agreement has been reached at the level of House leaders on the way to dispose of Bill C-125, an act to provide supplementary borrowing authority for the government. With Madam Speaker's permission, I will read the motion or House order. If you seek unanimous consent, I think we will obtain it. Therefore I move, seconded by the hon. member for Yukon (Mr. Nielsen) and the hon. member for Hamilton Mountain (Mr. Deans):
That, with regard to Bill C-125, an act to provide supplementary borrowing authority, the amendment proposed by the Leader of the Opposition be deemed to have been withdrawn;
That, when the said bill has been read a second time, it shall be referred to a Committee of the Whole;
That, at the appropriate time in Committee of the Whole stage of the said bill, a minister of the Crown shall propose an amendment to delete in line 14 of page one of the said bill the word "eleven" and to substitute therefor the word "seven";-

Mr. Speaker, the Minister of Labour (Mr. Caccia) just said we could go back 100 years. We do not have to go back 100 years, we can look at the government this year: inflation, 11 per cent; deficit $20 billion plus; unemployed, 1.3 million. That is this government's record this year. We do not have to look back 100 years, let us look at it right now. I thought, Mr. Speaker, I would never see the day that the Minister of Finance (Mr. MacEachen) would look bright in economic terms. I finally saw that tonight. I am glad the Minister of Labour is not taking over the finance portfolio;
Supplementary Borrowing Authority
we would be in even greater trouble than we are today, if that is possible.
